{
  "title": "Canonical Data Model — RED",
  "description": "Generic RED (request rate / errors / duration) for one selected service, source, and direction at a time. $source forces picking exactly one source type (spanmetrics / otel_semconv / envoy / grafana / prometheus) rather than 'All': the same underlying gRPC call can be reported by both the spanmetrics connector and native OTel RPC instrumentation simultaneously, so mixing sources risks double-counting the same request, the same class of issue as the frontend's overlapping GET/GET-with-route spans. The three inbound RED panels are aggregated (grouped by service/source only, no per-operation breakdown); a separate panel below breaks inbound traffic down by operation (via $operation), cdm_request_context, with cdm_request_type shown alongside it in the legend rather than as a separate filter. The three outbound RED panels are grouped by destination directly (cdm_request_context, cdm_request_type) instead of aggregating across destinations, so a single upstream causing errors or latency is visible without a separate panel. All three inbound panels overlay the anomaly-detection framework's bands and insights (as in the Anomalies dashboards), computed by rules/saafe/anomaly.yml's canonical_requests/canonical_error_ratio/canonical_latency_p95 rules -- generic across sources via group-by (service, cdm_source), not a filter on one. Latency shows p95 only, matching the anomaly rule it's overlaid with, normalized to seconds at query time: cdm_unit can't be converted by relabeling (rescaling a histogram bucket boundary needs arithmetic, not a label rewrite), so the panel computes the quantile separately per cdm_unit and scales the ms branch before combining -- the anomaly rule does the same. Requests/errors/duration are computed inline directly from series enriched with cdm_metric=\"requests_total\"/\"request_duration\" (no recording-rule layer for the canonical model itself, __name__ stays each source's own metric name). Note: request_type=\"internal\" series (no clear inbound/outbound side) don't appear in either row by design. A final Insights row (same state-timeline pattern as the Assertions dashboard) shows firing SAAFE alerts scoped to the selected $service; alerts that carry a cdm_source label are further scoped to the selected $source, while alerts without one (VersionUpdated, InstanceDown, InstanceRestarted, the saturation alerts) always show regardless of $source.",
  "uid": "cdm-red-saturation",
  "schemaVersion": 40,
  "version": 1,
  "editable": true,
  "graphTooltip": 1,
  "timezone": "browser",
  "fiscalYearStartMonth": 0,
  "liveNow": false,
  "refresh": "30s",
  "time": {
    "from": "now-1h",
    "to": "now"
  },
  "timepicker": {},
